          "text": "**Observations**\n\n*Input layer (W1).* Rows 0–1 of the image (inputs 0–55) and row 27 (756–783) carry nothing but small ±0.03 initialization-scale noise, as do the first ~2–3 and last ~3 columns of every row. Informative weights (|w| ≳ 0.1) first appear at image row 2–3 in the central columns ~10–22 (e.g. inputs 66–77, 94–107: unit 1 ≈ +0.12…+0.16, unit 14 ≈ −0.13…−0.16), and they persist all the way down to row 25 (inputs ~706–723) and row 26 (inputs ~736–749: units 4, 5 ≈ +0.1, unit 14 ≈ −0.1). In the middle rows the structured band spans columns ~2–24. So the \"used\" region is a tall vertical rectangle: rows 2–26, with the top rows narrow and centred, the middle rows almost full width, and the bottom rows narrowing again.\n\n*Hidden units.* Several units are essentially spatial templates:\n- Unit 1: positive on the top-centre (rows 2–6, cols 10–14) – \"something bright at the top of the image\".\n- Units 4 and 5: strong positives (0.20–0.25) in a vertical central stripe (cols ~11–13, rows 10–13) and again at the bottom rows; unit 5 strongly negative (−0.35) at the left middle.\n- Unit 3: strong positive (0.2–0.28) on a left-of-centre vertical column (cols 3–9, rows 13–18), negative (−0.24) at the centre of rows 11–13; unit 15 is nearly its negative on the same left strip.\n- Unit 0: positive (0.2–0.29) in the lower-left quadrant (rows 20–22, cols 8–12), negative in the upper-centre.\n- Unit 6: strong positive blob (0.29) around rows 16–17, cols 7–9, negative just above.\n- Unit 7: negative in the upper-left, positive in the lower-left (a low, flat, left-weighted shape).\n- Unit 14: negative along the whole top-centre and bottom-centre – \"image is tall\".\n\n*Output layer.* The hidden→output weights pair these templates with classes in a tidy way: the \"bright top\" unit 1 is positive for classes 2, 3, 6, 8 and strongly negative for 5, 7, 9 (and 4); the \"tall image\" unit 14 (negative when the bottom/top rows are lit) feeds class 3 with −1.15 and class 8/0 with +1.19/+0.78; the flat-low-left unit 7 drives class 7 (+1.44) and suppresses class 5 (−0.93); unit 6 pushes 4, 6, 5, 9 and suppresses 7, 1, 3. So classes {5, 7, 9} behave as a group (never lit at the top, flat shapes), classes {0, 2, 4, 6} as another (tall, filled top-centre), class 3 is the tallest, class 1 is distinguished by central-stripe units.\n\n**Reasoning**\n\nA 28×28 grayscale, 10-class dataset points to the MNIST family. MNIST, EMNIST-digits and KMNIST are all produced by fitting the glyph into a 20×20 box and centring it in a 28×28 frame, so rows 0–3 and 24–27 are essentially always zero and a trained first layer shows no structure there. Here the first layer has clearly learned weights at image rows 2, 3, 25 and 26, which rules those out. The used area is a tall, vertically oriented region: narrow at the top (shoulders/neck/waistband), full width in the middle, narrower at the bottom (hems/legs) – the silhouette envelope of clothing items, not of handwritten strokes. The learned templates match garment-vs-footwear statistics: a group of three classes that are never bright at the top and are low, flat, left-heavy shapes (shoes), a group of upper-body garments with a filled top-centre, one class with a central vertical stripe/gap (trousers), one very tall class (dress), and one class that is short and boxy and favoured when the top and bottom rows are dark (bag). The class indices that fall into these groups (5/7/9 footwear, 0/2/4/6 tops, 1 trousers, 3 dress, 8 bag) are exactly the Fashion-MNIST label order. notMNIST or CIFAR-style data are ruled out (letters fill the full frame including corners; CIFAR is 32×32 colour).\n\n**Dataset:** Fashion-MNIST (Zalando article images, 28×28 grayscale, 10 classes).\n\n**Classes:** 0 T-shirt/top, 1 Trouser, 2 Pullover, 3 Dress, 4 Coat, 5 Sandal, 6 Shirt, 7 Sneaker, 8 Bag, 9 Ankle boot.\n\n**Confidence:** 85%",
